#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 480.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069651} :activity/id #uuid "484aecae-ce38-5933-8c1b-d7a4869c93b3" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048369 :topic/id #uuid "99e1fe28-92a1-545f-8ee9-b78506123551" :topic/math-academy-id 469} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069968 :tutorial/content "A **root** of a polynomial is a value that will make the polynomial equal zero when substituted for the polynomial's variable.\n\nFor example, consider the polynomial $x^2-9.$ This polynomial has two roots, $x=3$ and $x=-3,$ because the polynomial evaluates to $0$ when either of these values is substituted for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3\\,⇒\\,{x}^{2}-9 & = \\\\ (3)^{2}-9 & = \\\\ 9-9 & = \\\\ 0 & \\,✓ \\\\ x=-3\\,⇒\\,{x}^{2}-9 & = \\\\ (-3)^{2}-9 & = \\\\ 9-9 & = \\\\ 0 & \\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, a number like $x=1$ is *not* a root of the polynomial because the polynomial does not evaluate to $0$ when this value is substituted for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=1\\,⇒\\,{x}^{2}-9 & = \\\\ (1)^{2}-9 & = \\\\ 1-9 & = \\\\ -8 & \\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5b765b45-ae0c-5c2f-958b-e69d41100abc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3321 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "7bf976d5-f5da-5111-a8db-afd7667d6464" :step/math-academy-id 18472 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069652}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069652 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068749 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068582 :question/id #uuid "4968972d-3bc9-5100-aa70-46213e0c3581" :question/math-academy-id "e-5028"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b680bd3-e1e3-5ff5-ade3-39977cbdaaf7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049472}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069316} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552435} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552497}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values that are Roots of a Given Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "43f1293f-5871-5104-98bf-b58f5a85bbdc" :step/math-academy-id 18473 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069653}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069653 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069969 :tutorial/content "A **parametric polynomial** is a polynomial in which one of the coefficients is itself represented as another variable.\n\nFor example, the polynomial below is a parametric polynomial:\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x^2 -px\n$$\n\nHere, $p$ is a **parameter**,  which means that it corresponds to a certain constant value. Unlike the variable $x,$ the parameter is treated exactly like any other coefficient. It's only special in that we don't know its numerical value.\n\nIf we're given the root of a polynomial, we can find the numerical value of a parameter. Let's say, for instance, that a root of the above polynomial is $x=1.$ This means that the polynomial is zero for that value:\n\n$$\nf(1)=0\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate the polynomial at $x=1,$ set it equal to $0,$ and solve the equation for the parameter $p\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1) & =0 \\\\ 2(1)^{2}-p(1) & =0 \\\\ 2-p & =0 \\\\ p & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "91fb2858-964a-50e9-a2fc-ae2af84b6b20" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3322 :tutorial/title "Solving for an Unknown Parameter Given the Root of a Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "b2f6b6b9-edee-5a14-9900-fb89f4ef499a" :step/math-academy-id 18475 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069654}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068750 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068583 :question/id #uuid "c1830274-3260-5bf5-aef1-c855a89efbf2" :question/math-academy-id "e-5030"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9328cc9a-2e6c-5ac2-bae4-f02c86fe9d29" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069060} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186514421} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522760} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522789}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Parameter Given the Root of a Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "7174685e-b638-5eb0-a858-a847b1762b27" :step/math-academy-id 18476}] :activity/title "Determining the Roots of Polynomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068994}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522825} :activity/id #uuid "e7cdfaae-9f34-5e4f-854e-c1d607458903" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048369 :topic/id #uuid "99e1fe28-92a1-545f-8ee9-b78506123551" :topic/math-academy-id 469} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522822 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522789 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8141248f-ae02-59d5-9985-216116e2a8e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522278} {:answer/id #uuid "a5e33f54-5f9e-5175-b0c7-6035f1211b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522370} {:answer/id #uuid "c30cf463-743f-5343-9b3b-e2b54d327e16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522428} {:answer/id #uuid "d4dbce40-8e28-5d99-a914-1fc7bae9ef3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522461} {:answer/id #uuid "de6f2964-ee7a-5958-a213-8d1216e4287b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "k=-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522487}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522428} :answer-field/id #uuid "59e0a079-1230-556b-88e6-09520bd04216" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522606}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d718b4fe-ac83-5ded-9cff-9bf61a11c436" :question/math-academy-id "q-14238" :question/problem "Find $k$ if $2$ is a root of $f(x)={x}^{4}+k{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}-57x+6k.$" :question/worked-solution "Since  $x=2$ is a root, we have $f(2)=0.$ Therefore, we can evaluate the polynomial at $x=2,$ set it equal to $0,$ and solve the equation for the parameter $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)^{4}+k(2)^{3}-7(2)^{2}-57(2)+6k & =0 \\\\ 16+8k-28-114+6k & =0 \\\\ 14k-126 & =0 \\\\ 14k & =126 \\\\ k & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "149763fa-fcc2-55ff-bbcd-e8ae22afe33e"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522825 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522760 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "027f0819-c021-5635-b7d1-21f53a81eb36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522018} {:answer/id #uuid "16c11ec4-ff0c-5c06-86de-a5fdbf8b9db1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522067} {:answer/id #uuid "2bc0013d-319f-5abd-b000-4099afd2ca7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522117} {:answer/id #uuid "a670b93b-9bea-55ed-97fc-24e49d5de5cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522371} {:answer/id #uuid "c210b6d6-4799-5836-860c-3d8f72c0f36e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522425}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522067} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a0a44b2-6e7c-58bd-8a08-7b91d747ebd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522636}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "940f3759-8774-576e-891c-0f76989295c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-14194" :question/problem "Find $p$ if $-2$ is a root of $f(x)=5{x}^{2}+6x-2p.$" :question/worked-solution "Since  $x=-2$ is a root, we have $f(-2)=0.$ Therefore, we can evaluate the polynomial at $x=-2,$ set it equal to $0,$ and solve the equation for the parameter $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(-2)^{2}+6(-2)-2p & =0 \\\\ 20-12-2p & =0 \\\\ 8-2p & =0 \\\\ -2p & =-8 \\\\ p & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "23d9f834-3911-510e-bae1-ec6c30e48281" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522886}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522886 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522786 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "127d7eca-f156-5581-bb3f-5909c3e3a681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522057} {:answer/id #uuid "2f612688-2f3d-57bb-9415-7a91d68a847c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522124} {:answer/id #uuid "5b55e4a9-5374-5c37-bb1e-cee774d9b81e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522206} {:answer/id #uuid "74efa23a-d4f4-5131-99b2-7c1b585da360" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522250} {:answer/id #uuid "f9994e31-f0b7-5d6f-8912-7380e35bb9c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "p=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522547}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522206} :answer-field/id #uuid "075758a5-0cef-58d0-bf2e-50db1a582ad6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522558}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d2b7c3bc-8988-5881-96d5-877a27dfd407" :question/math-academy-id "q-14203" :question/problem "Find $p$ if $-2$ is a root of $f(x)=3p{x}^{2}-21px-18.$" :question/worked-solution "Since  $x=-2$ is a root, we have $f(-2)=0.$ Therefore, we can evaluate the polynomial at $x=-2,$ set it equal to $0,$ and solve the equation for the parameter $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p(-2)^{2}-21p(-2)-18 & =0 \\\\ 12p+42p-18 & =0 \\\\ 54p-18 & =0 \\\\ 54p & =18 \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "df6a6199-b720-581e-9425-82f1d00b1651" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186522822}}] :activity/title "Determining the Roots of Polynomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186522013}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552590} :activity/id #uuid "f8ac1563-5b5a-58a7-95b1-1bfd5312746c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048369 :topic/id #uuid "99e1fe28-92a1-545f-8ee9-b78506123551" :topic/math-academy-id 469} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552549 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552497 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ed4f90b-7446-5fe1-a027-5e3a5ce07b55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551892} {:answer/id #uuid "6a45a887-7bd4-55cc-9567-0be073fde218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551911} {:answer/id #uuid "7d5b8ea2-92f3-534a-af6b-e41b17d163cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551963} {:answer/id #uuid "871e3b59-d5c2-5e57-ac83-1e3ea351c7c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551992} {:answer/id #uuid "e70da112-f9be-571f-9779-eff48a54af1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552203}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551992} :answer-field/id #uuid "670a06f6-a994-5703-aa13-cc5f486be4c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b7f2915e-8d70-5b64-9329-25d718df5c40" :question/math-academy-id "q-37656" :question/problem "Which of the following is a root of the polynomial ${x}^{3}-6{x}^{2}+9x+1?$\n\nI. $x=-6$\n\nII. $x=0$\n\nIII. $x=2$" :question/worked-solution "- Substituting $-6$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-6)^{3}-6(-6)^{2}+9(-6)+1 & = \\\\ -216-216-54+1 & = \\\\ -485\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $-6$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $-6$ is *not*  a root.\n\n- Substituting $0$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0)^{3}-6(0)^{2}+9(0)+1 & = \\\\ 0-0+0+1 & = \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $0$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $0$ is *not*  a root.\n\n- Substituting $2$ for $x$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)^{3}-6(2)^{2}+9(2)+1 & = \\\\ 8-24+18+1 & = \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $2$ is substituted for $x.$ Therefore, $2$ is *not*  a root.\n\nTherefore, there are no roots among the given options, so the correct answer is \"None\"."} :step/id #uuid "3665dc42-60d5-515f-84ce-302acbb58230"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552435 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a9ad680-ea7c-5349-8099-ee0f7360edf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551722} {:answer/id #uuid "1f2cf0d6-8ae2-54dc-8299-3d8f8c1181f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551731} {:answer/id #uuid "3b455006-8a18-50b3-9dfd-7f67b1c755e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551809} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6327b9-2262-5b77-8b24-265e4673cdf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552076} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1cf656-c57d-5c73-a614-b3fbde117e8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552155}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186551731} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7315726-3016-58de-b132-f1d5a281014b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552370}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "492f172a-6ec8-53da-b873-64a863435b9b" :question/math-academy-id "q-3896" :question/problem "Which of the following is a root of the polynomial ${u}^{4}-26{u}^{2}+25?$\n\nI. $u=1$\n\nII. $u=5$\n\nIII. $u=3$" :question/worked-solution "- Substituting $1$ for $u$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}^{4}-26(1)^{2}+25 & = \\\\ 1-26(1)+25 & = \\\\ 1-26+25 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial is $0$ when $1$ is substituted for $u.$ Therefore, $1$ is a root.\n\n- Substituting $5$ for $u$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(5)^{4}-26(5)^{2}+25 & = \\\\ 625-26(25)+25 & = \\\\ 625-650+25 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial is $0$ when $5$ is substituted for $u.$ Therefore, $5$ is a root.\n\n- Substituting $3$ for $u$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3)^{4}-26(3)^{2}+25 & = \\\\ 81-26(9)+25 & = \\\\ 81-234+25 & = \\\\ -128\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $3$ is substituted for $u.$ Therefore, $3$ is *not*  a root.\n\nTherefore, the roots are $u=1$ and $u=5,$ so the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "53efb6fc-4790-57dc-b467-c06c75735221" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552549}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552590 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552428 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3aeb3eef-29a8-5ea6-b548-ddcb20776a00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551807} {:answer/id #uuid "487830ca-ce0a-5e85-8c66-6e544da4f594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551841} {:answer/id #uuid "67785bcb-303e-5072-8f88-6d857e799dc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551906} {:answer/id #uuid "78a5611f-f5c6-5764-bfa1-f8b3d4886119" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I  and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551949} {:answer/id #uuid "adb4124d-f77d-5b00-b0f0-2fbbee5b7d24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552082}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552082} :answer-field/id #uuid "52ee438e-ac14-5d2d-9ba2-e23f74a8dc8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552301}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4251c35b-5737-5f81-8168-9bb04b9bfceb" :question/math-academy-id "q-3894" :question/problem "Which of the following is a root of the polynomial ${v}^{4}-4{v}^{3}+7v-28?$\n\nI. $v=-4$\n\nII. $v=4$\n\nIII. $v=2$" :question/worked-solution "- Substituting $-4$ for $v$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4)^{4}-4(-4)^{3}+7(-4)-28 & = \\\\ 256+256-28-28 & = \\\\ 456\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $-4$ is substituted for $v.$ Therefore, $-4$ is *not*  a root.\n\n- Substituting $4$ for $v$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4)^{4}-4(4)^{3}+7(4)-28 & = \\\\ 256-256+28-28 & = \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial is $0$ when $4$ is substituted for $v.$ Therefore, $4$ is a root.\n\n- Substituting $2$ for $v$ in the polynomial and evaluating, we get the following result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2)^{4}-4(2)^{3}+7(2)-28 & = \\\\ 16-32+14-28 & = \\\\ -30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe polynomial does *not* equal $0$ when $2$ is substituted for $v.$ Therefore, $2$ is *not*  a root.\n\nTherefore, the root is $v=4,$ so the correct answer is \"II only\"."} :step/id #uuid "a84e39c6-3002-5b16-8eaf-28f7699dae4d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186552560}}] :activity/title "Determining the Roots of Polynomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186551664}]}
